    "note": "Engrossed (21 RS HB 175/GA). House passed 73-24 on 2021-03-03; later amendments are not in the text read. Borderline gun_policy: protects the firearms trade from financial and government boycotts rather than regulating guns themselves. LegiScan desc mentions a civil action by victims; the text read has only the Attorney General action."
  },
  "operative_text": "Section 2 (new section of KRS Chapter 237): It shall be unlawful for a financial institution or government entity to adopt a policy that it will not do business with a manufacturer, retailer, or distributor of firearms, firearms accessories, or ammunition; a shooting range; or a trade association solely because the manufacturer, retailer, distributor, shooting range, or trade association supports or is engaged in the lawful commerce of firearms, firearms accessories, or ammunition products.\nSection 3 (new section of KRS Chapter 237): The Attorney General may file a civil cause of action in District Court in the name of the Commonwealth against a financial institution or government entity believed to be engaging in or that has previously engaged in an unlawful discriminatory practice under Section 2 of this Act to: (1) Obtain a declaratory judgment that the act or practice violates Section 2 of this Act; (2) Enjoin any act or practice that violates Section 2 of this Act by seeking a temporary restraining order or preliminary or permanent injunction, without bond, upon the giving of appropriate notice; or (3) Recover civil penalties of up to ten thousand dollars ($10,000) per violation of Section 2 of this Act."
}